 - **Street rules for the place, editable streets and street names:** **Site parameters › Street rules** has the AI read the place's subdivision rules and drainage criteria (the city's, the county's, or a city's ETJ), each value with a link to its source, the ones it isn't sure of marked to confirm, and Waller County's rules standing in for what it can't find. The street widths, blocks, cul-de-sacs, lot sizes, building lines, entrances and detention then follow the sheet, and the sheet is shared with the team so the next plan in that place starts with it. It is the AI's reading of the code, never a compliance claim. Select a neighbourhood street to **Make It a Cul-de-sac**, **Delete This Stretch**, **Curve It** or **Re-Lay This Block** (its local streets another way, the rest of the plan exactly as it was); dragging a street's point bends it smoothly and keeps the streets meeting it joined. Turn on **Street names** and every street gets a name from a theme ("golf", "Texas rivers"), never one already on a road nearby, shown along the streets on the map and the PDF; rename any in the Selected box. Change With AI understands "make this street a cul-de-sac", "curve the main street", "re-lay this block" and "name the streets after Texas rivers". Plans without a rule sheet lay out exactly as before.
 - **Neighbourhoods laid out like real subdivisions:** single-family layouts on 8 acres or more now come with streets drawn the way master-planned subdivisions are: a loop road one lot in from the edge or a main street, curving local streets meeting it at T's, loop lanes and cul-de-sacs, the pond at the back with a trail round it (or a lake and the commons in the middle), and lots marched along every curve with wedge lots round the cul-de-sacs. They're offered with the parallel streets of before and go first when they hold about as many homes (often more). Blocks, cul-de-sacs and junction spacing follow typical street rules, and the checks warn when an edit breaks one. Re-fits keep the streets and lay the homes out again along them; a street bent by hand gets its lots re-flowed, a deleted home leaves its lot empty, and a park dropped on a street cuts it back to cul-de-sacs either side. Homes pods of a mixed plan are offered them as well, but keep straight streets unless subdivision streets hold about as many homes (on most sites they don't yet). **Settings › Neighbourhood streets** picks the style.
 - **Mixed sites in pods:** big mixed sites are now also laid out the way master-planned communities are drawn: a loop or spine road first, with the main entry boulevard and greenbelts, cutting the site into pods, each with one use and its own street direction. Say where things go ("retail at the corner of FM 362 and Bauer Road, apartments along FM 362, homes around a lake, the commons in the middle") and the pods follow it; the notes say which placements were met. These layouts are ranked with the usual zones-from-the-street ones and only go first when they keep the program (Settings › Mixed sites picks either or both). Select a piece to **Re-lay This Pod** or **Swap With…** another pod; Change With AI understands "re-lay the apartments" and "swap the apartments and the retail". Re-fits lay out only the pod you changed. Streets inside each pod are straight, or laid out like a subdivision where that holds about as many homes.
